X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/from-pizza-to-plant-leaves-the-mathematics-of-thin-materials-carolina-science-cafe"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261121T064455Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54427189378492
DTSTART:20261201T230000Z
DTEND:20261202T000000Z
DESCRIPTION:Why do plant leaves curl as they grow or dry out? Why do you fo
 ld a slice of pizza when picking it up? How can inflatable structures supp
 ort heavy loads? At December’s Carolina Science Café\, Dr. Thomas Chand
 ler will explore these questions through the lens of applied mathematics. 
 He will introduce the mechanics of thin elastic materials\, showing how ge
 ometry and the competition between bending and stretching govern their beh
 avior. From Gauss’s remarkable theorem to the design of soft robots\, he
  will demonstrate how these mathematical ideas help us understand biologic
 al structures and inspire new technologies.\n\n \n\n Free and open to adul
 ts\n Light appetizers available for attendees\n Drinks available for purch
 ase\n\n \n\nHosted by Morehead Planetarium at Haw River Tap & Table in Car
 rboro.
GEO:35.910154;-79.068833
LOCATION:Haw River Tap & Table
SUMMARY:From Pizza to Plant Leaves: The Mathematics of Thin Materials | Car
 olina Science Café
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/from-pizza-to-plant-leaves-the
 -mathematics-of-thin-materials-carolina-science-cafe
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar Group Gregg Museum Of Art Design": "https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/intergeneration-story-circle"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_group_gregg_museum_of_art_design
X-SOURCE:NC State Gregg Museum of Art & Design
DTSTAMP:20261121T065036Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54505683272884
DTSTART:20261201T233000Z
DTEND:20261202T013000Z
DESCRIPTION:As part of the Gregg Museum of Art & Design's ongoing explorati
 on of sanctuary\, community memory and collective care\, community histori
 an and cultural organizer Johnny Lee Chapman III will organize and facilit
 ate a series of intergenerational Story Circles. Centered on the voices of
  Black leaders and elders in Raleigh\, these gatherings are designed to co
 nnect deeply with young adults — the next generation of leaders.\n\nThro
 ugh facilitated dialogue and storytelling\, the Story Circles are inspired
  by the exhibitions at the Gregg Museum — Crafting Sanctuaries: Black Sp
 aces of the Great Depression South and Community Stories: Sustaining North
  Carolina’s Black Heritage — that will be on display during the Story 
 Circles. This series will explore how Black communities in Raleigh built n
 etworks of mutual support and sanctuary. These gatherings will create powe
 rful opportunities for Black elders and leaders to pass down lived histori
 es to NC State students and the broader community\, strengthening intergen
 erational dialogue and preserving vital community memory.\n\nJoin the muse
 um and Chapman for the Dec. 1 Story Circle\, featuring Carmen Wimberley Ca
 uthen\, a Raleigh native\, historian\, author and founder of Research and 
 Resource\, a historical research and community engagement firm.\n\nThis ev
 ent is free\, but registration is required. Space is limited to preserve t
 he intimacy of the circle.
GEO:35.784762;-78.661636
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design
SUMMARY:Intergeneration Story Circle
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/intergeneration-story-circle
CATEGORIES:Lectures and Talks
CATEGORIES:Community Events
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events 1 Ics": "https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/mecklenburgs-declaration-ncs-revolutionary-presbyterians"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_1_ics
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20261121T065024Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53178468630085
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261202
DESCRIPTION:An article published in the April 30\, 1819 edition of the Rale
 igh Register and North Carolina Gazette proclaimed the following: “It is
  not probably known to many of our readers\, that the citizens of Mecklenb
 urg County\, in this State\, made a Declaration of Independence more than 
 a year before Congress made theirs. The following Document on the subject 
 has lately come to the hands of the Editor from unquestionable authority\,
   and is published that it may go down to posterity.”  \n\nThe document 
 in question was called the Mecklenburg Declaration of Independence\, and i
 t had allegedly declared Mecklenburg “free and independent” from Briti
 sh rule on May 20\, 1775. Of the 27 men who were said to have signed the d
 ocument\, almost all were Presbyterian.\n\nBut many people were confused 
 – why were they just now hearing about this revolutionary action? The or
 iginal document burned in a house fire in 1800\, and the published version
  had been reconstructed from notes made by the clerk of the 1775 meeting. 
 No extant copies could be found.\n\nThe debate over the authenticity of th
 e Mecklenburg Declaration began. And\, when a copy of a document called th
 e Mecklenburg Resolves written on May 30\, 1775\, was discovered in a 1775
  newspaper\, the debate intensified. The Resolves were revolutionary\, but
  stopped short of declaring independence. Did both documents exist? Or was
  the Declaration being conflated with the Resolves?\n\nExamine the evidenc
 e and decide for yourself by visiting the new exhibit\, “Mecklenburg Dec
 laration?” at the Presbyterian Heritage Center.\n\nThe PHC is open Tuesd
 ay-Friday (10-4) and Saturday (12-4).
GEO:35.646737;-82.300463
LOCATION:Presbyterian Heritage Center
SUMMARY:Mecklenburg's Declaration: NC's Revolutionary Presbyterians
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/mecklenburgs-declaration-ncs
 -revolutionary-presbyterians
CATEGORIES:Exhibits & Tours
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
